The Guess → Guess → Guess Analogy: Why AI Drifts (and Why Guardrails Fail)

Most explanations of AI hallucinations are complicated.
They don’t need to be.

Here’s the simplest possible explanation of why AI drifts — and it’s the same mechanism humans use when thinking.



Humans Think by Guessing First, Verifying Later

When a human doesn’t know something, they make a guess.

Then they make another guess based on the first.
Then another.
Then another.

It looks like this:

1. Guess
2. Build on the guess
3. Build on the build
4. Build on the build of the build


This chain creates creativity — but it also creates drift.

Humans stay stable because they verify later:

• “Was my guess right?”
• “Does this still make sense?”
• “Should I correct this?”


Verification stabilises the chain.



AI Copies the Guessing — But Not the Verifying

AI does the same first part:

1. Guess
2. Build on the guess
3. Build on the build
4. Build on the build of the build


But AI never verifies.

It treats every guess as a fact.

So the chain drifts:

• small assumption → becomes context
• context → becomes foundation
• foundation → becomes “truth”
• “truth” → becomes the next guess


This is structural drift.

It’s not a bug.
It’s not bad training.
It’s not a dataset issue.

It’s how inference works.



Why Guardrails Fail

Guardrails try to fix the final output.

But drift happens inside the chain, long before the guardrail sees anything.

By the time the guardrail checks the answer:

• the drift already happened
• the assumptions already stacked
• the internal state already shifted
• the reasoning already diverged


Guardrails can’t reach inside the chain.
So they fail.



Why This Matters

This simple mechanism explains:

• hallucinations
• contradictions
• unstable long‑form reasoning
• loss of constraints
• context collapse
• “wandering” chain‑of‑thought
• why identical prompts produce different answers


It’s all the same root cause:

